<%# A section's heading and standfirst, set the same way everywhere.

  WHY THIS EXISTS. `cards`, `catalogue` and `faq` each printed their own `h2` and `p` with their own classes,
  so the page's section rhythm depended on three partials agreeing by coincidence. They did not: the home
  page's three sections opened at the same size with the same gap, and nothing told a reader where one ended
  and the next began. One partial, one rule above the heading, one measure for the standfirst.

  THE RULE IS THE SECTION BOUNDARY, SO IT IS NOT CONDITIONAL. It used to be emitted inside the guard below,
  which meant a block carrying neither heading nor standfirst got no rule and no padding -- and that is
  exactly the shape every index page opens with (`Marketing::ContentBlueprint#index_page` is one catalogue
  block with no copy of its own, because the page's H1 already names it). The section step above it then read
  as a 92px void with nothing in it. Only the padding belongs to the heading, because without a heading there
  is nothing to pad away from.
%>
<div class="border-t border-lyn-line <%= 'pt-8' if heading.present? || lede.present? %>">
  <% if heading.present? %>
    <h2 class="text-balance text-2xl font-semibold leading-[1.4] tracking-tight text-lyn-ink sm:text-3xl">
      <%= heading %>
    </h2>
  <% end %>
  <% if lede.present? %>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-base leading-[1.85] text-lyn-ink-soft"><%= lede %></p>
  <% end %>
</div>
